WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:05.015
Every year on July 14, France celebrates Bastille Day.

2
00:00:05.016 --> 00:00:16.397
Parades fill the streets of Paris; French soldiers march down its most famous avenue, the Champs-Élysées; and the country's politicians speak to cheering crowds.

3
00:00:16.398 --> 00:00:26.827
The holiday commemorates the day in 1789 when a crowd of French citizens stormed the Bastille, an old fortress being used as a prison.

4
00:00:26.828 --> 00:00:32.494
Why do the French celebrate an attack on a poorly guarded and nearly empty prison?

5
00:00:32.495 --> 00:00:38.703
They celebrate it because the fall of the Bastille marks the start of the French Revolution.

6
00:00:38.704 --> 00:00:45.267
Before the French Revolution, King Louis XVI ruled France in an absolute monarchy.

7
00:00:45.268 --> 00:00:50.982
With the support of its nobles and the Catholic Church, he had total control of the country.

8
00:00:50.983 --> 00:00:58.675
The people of France were legally organized into three social classes: clergy, nobility, and commoners.

9
00:00:58.676 --> 00:01:07.851
The clergy and nobility made up about 3 percent of the population and held the highest positions in the church, the army, and the government.

10
00:01:07.852 --> 00:01:17.616
The clergy collected about 10 percent of French citizens' incomes and production but did not pay taxes on their own extensive land holdings.

11
00:01:17.617 --> 00:01:25.401
The nobles collected rent and taxes from the peasants who lived on their lands but paid no taxes to the crown themselves.

12
00:01:25.402 --> 00:01:30.384
The remaining 97 percent of the population of France were commoners.

13
00:01:30.385 --> 00:01:36.056
This group consisted of peasants and a growing middle class called the bourgeoisie.

14
00:01:36.057 --> 00:01:42.937
The commoners usually paid a substantial portion of their incomes to the nobles, the clergy, and the crown.

15
00:01:42.938 --> 00:01:46.921
But in the eighteenth century, French society was changing.

16
00:01:46.922 --> 00:01:52.359
Many of its members wanted to create a government run by the people instead of a monarch.

17
00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:58.144
The members of the bourgeoisie wanted to have more rights and pay fewer taxes.

18
00:01:58.145 --> 00:02:02.808
They had become wealthy in business, but could not hold titles or land.

19
00:02:02.809 --> 00:02:07.639
They also did not have the hereditary privileges allowed to the nobility.

20
00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:13.409
Furthermore, the bourgeoisie was well educated but had little say in running the country.

21
00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:17.019
A growing intellectual movement fueled their resentment.

22
00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:25.723
The writers and thinkers of the Enlightenment believed in the ideals of reason, freedom, progress, and greater rights for commoners.

23
00:02:25.724 --> 00:02:33.628
France's poor economy in the 1780s also encouraged revolution. The country was on the brink of bankruptcy.

24
00:02:33.629 --> 00:02:41.606
Several recent wars, as well as support for the American Revolution against Britain, had depleted the country's finances.

25
00:02:41.607 --> 00:02:50.614
Harsh winters and failed crops had led to famines, and basic foods—bread in particular—were unavailable or unaffordable.

26
00:02:50.615 --> 00:02:56.208
The high levels of unemployment plaguing the country only aggravated the situation.

27
00:02:56.209 --> 00:03:01.062
But France's royalty did not react to the plight of its commoners.

28
00:03:01.063 --> 00:03:11.303
In 1787, to ease the financial crisis, Louis XVI's finance minister proposed a tax on property owned by the nobles and clergy.

29
00:03:11.304 --> 00:03:17.586
They rejected the tax and, instead, called for a rare assembly of the Estates-General.

30
00:03:17.587 --> 00:03:27.347
This meeting consisted of representatives of the clergy (the First Estate), the nobility (the Second Estate), and the commoners (the Third Estate).

31
00:03:27.348 --> 00:03:36.979
As news of the coming assembly spread, people's hopes for reform grew, and lists of grievances were sent to the king from across the country.

32
00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:48.232
In May 1789 at the Palace of Versailles, the lavish home of Louis XVI, the Estates-General finally convened but quickly came to a deadlock.

33
00:03:48.233 --> 00:03:55.311
The Third Estate represented over 90 percent of the population and an increasing amount of France's wealth.

34
00:03:55.313 --> 00:04:01.188
As a result, its members insisted that their votes be given more weight than in the past.

35
00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:07.393
The First and Second Estates refused to make the change, and no decisions were made for weeks.

36
00:04:07.395 --> 00:04:16.519
On June 17, 1789, the Third Estate decided to proceed in its task without the input of the clergy and nobles.

37
00:04:16.521 --> 00:04:32.591
It declared itself a National Assembly, with full authority to resolve France's financial troubles. In response, the king quickly closed the assembly hall where the National Assembly met, but its members continued to meet in an indoor tennis court.

38
00:04:32.593 --> 00:04:39.609
There, they all pledged an oath not to separate until they had created a new constitution for France.

39
00:04:39.611 --> 00:04:43.915
The people of Paris were following the events at Versailles closely.

40
00:04:43.917 --> 00:04:54.587
When they learned that Louis XVI had ordered the army to gather around Versailles and Paris, they feared the king was about to take control of the National Assembly by force.

41
00:04:54.588 --> 00:05:04.658
News of the army's arrival incited riots in the streets of Paris, and on July 14, 1789, a mob approached the Bastille.

42
00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:09.012
The old fortress was where France's royalty imprisoned its enemies.

43
00:05:09.013 --> 00:05:16.521
To Parisians, it was a symbol of tyranny and oppression by an uncaring government that did not answer to anyone.

44
00:05:16.522 --> 00:05:22.733
It also contained a supply of gunpowder and weapons that would be useful for the revolutionaries.

45
00:05:22.734 --> 00:05:29.844
As the leaders of the mob negotiated with the Bastille's commander, shots were fired and fighting broke out.

46
00:05:29.845 --> 00:05:35.994
The mob broke into the fortress, freed its seven prisoners, and seized the weapons within.

47
00:05:35.995 --> 00:05:42.667
As a message to the king, the crowd hacked off the commander's head and displayed it on the end of a spear.

48
00:05:42.668 --> 00:05:45.056
The French Revolution had begun.

49
00:05:45.057 --> 00:05:49.650
The storming of the Bastille showed the people's support for the National Assembly.

50
00:05:49.651 --> 00:05:54.698
The uprising spread across France, and many nobles fled the country.

51
00:05:54.699 --> 00:06:00.365
In August, the assembly published the "Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen."

52
00:06:00.366 --> 00:06:04.538
The main principles were liberty, equality, and fraternity.

53
00:06:04.539 --> 00:06:13.141
All French people were now subject to the same laws, had the same privileges, and could no longer be imprisoned without cause.

54
00:06:13.142 --> 00:06:21.227
The years after the storming of the Bastille were full of unrest. Political parties formed and battled for control of the country.

55
00:06:21.228 --> 00:06:28.169
Power struggles continued as the assembly strove to establish a new constitution and a working government.

56
00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:33.947
In 1792, the monarchy had ended, and a new republic had been born.

57
00:06:33.948 --> 00:06:41.887
France went to war with Austria, Prussia, Britain, and Spain, nations whose monarchs were loyal to the king of France.

58
00:06:41.888 --> 00:06:50.381
The turmoil within France reached a horrific extreme when a revolutionary named Maximilien Robespierre gained power.

59
00:06:50.382 --> 00:06:56.771
Under the leadership of Robespierre, France entered a yearlong period known as the Reign of Terror.

60
00:06:56.772 --> 00:07:02.975
Anyone could be sentenced to death on the slightest suspicion of being against the National Assembly.

61
00:07:02.976 --> 00:07:12.914
Louis XVI was beheaded for treason on January 21, 1793, and Marie Antoinette was beheaded nine months later.

62
00:07:12.915 --> 00:07:23.146
From 1793 to 1794, as many as twenty thousand people were led through jeering mobs and executed at the guillotine.

63
00:07:23.147 --> 00:07:28.684
Fearing that they would be next, the leaders of the revolution began accusing one another.

64
00:07:28.685 --> 00:07:35.580
Robespierre himself was put to death, and a new government, called the Directory, came into power.

65
00:07:35.581 --> 00:07:45.565
The new government failed to unite the country. In 1799, the general of France's army, Napoleon Bonaparte, overthrew the Directory.

66
00:07:45.566 --> 00:07:50.400
He became a dictator and declared himself emperor in 1804.

67
00:07:50.401 --> 00:07:59.840
It would take many, many more years of war and political struggle before the republic's ideals of liberty, equality, and fraternity were realized.

68
00:07:59.841 --> 00:08:03.546
The ideal of democracy, however, had taken hold.

69
00:08:03.547 --> 00:08:11.317
The French Revolution's dramatic and bloody overthrow of the monarchy signaled that the will of the people should not be ignored.

70
00:08:11.318 --> 00:08:19.826
The principles promoted by this revolution have inspired people all over the world to fight for freedom and human rights.

